var ARCHIVE=[{"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000001", "title": "BST Overview: What It Is and Why It Matters", "d": "The BST: a binary tree where order lives in the structure itself.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nA binary search tree is a binary tree with one invariant: for every node, all keys in its left subtree are less than the node's key, and all keys in its right subtree are greater. That's the whole idea — and it turns the tree into a sorted container: search walks down choosing left/right like binary search on an array, insert finds the empty spot the search would end at, and inorder traversal reads keys in sorted order for free.\n\nBSTs matter because they're the simplest structure with ordered operations: search, insert, delete, min, max, predecessor, successor — all O(h) for height h, which is O(log n) when the tree stays balanced. They're the conceptual parent of every ordered map: database indexes, language runtimes' TreeMaps, and the balanced variants (AVL, red-black) that guarantee the height.\n\nThe shadow over the BST is degeneracy: insert sorted keys 1,2,3,4,5 and you get a chain — a linked list wearing a tree costume, O(n) everything. The plain BST is brilliant but fragile; the balanced variants exist to protect its promise. Learn the plain BST cold — property, operations, deletion's three cases — and the balanced trees become 'a BST plus rebalancing.'\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "overview", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000002", "title": "BST: How It Works — Core Operations", "d": "Search, insert, and delete guided by the ordering invariant.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nSearch(key): compare with the node — equal means found; smaller goes left, larger goes right; null means absent. Each step descends one level: O(h) time, O(1) space iterative (O(h) recursive). Insert(key): search for where it would be; attach the new node at the null link where the search ends — O(h). Both are binary search generalized from arrays to trees, which is why the name fits.\n\nDelete is the famous one with three cases: (1) leaf — just remove it; (2) one child — bypass it (link parent directly to the child); (3) two children — replace with the inorder successor (min of right subtree) or predecessor (max of left), then delete that successor (which has at most one child — case 1 or 2). All O(h). Min/max walk to the extreme leaf (O(h)); successor/predecessor climb or descend (O(h)). Inorder traversal yields sorted order in O(n). The operations are simple; the invariant is everything — every one of them preserves 'left \u003c node \u003c right' or the tree silently corrupts.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "operations": [{"op": "search", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "compare and descend; binary search on a tree"}, {"op": "insert", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "search to the null link, attach"}, {"op": "delete", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "three cases: leaf / one child / two children"}, {"op": "min / max", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "walk to the extreme leaf"}, {"op": "inorder", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "yields keys in sorted order"}], "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000003", "title": "BST: Internals, Trade-offs and When to Use It", "d": "Height is destiny: the balanced promise and the degenerate threat.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nInternally a BST node is data + left + right (+ parent in some designs). The entire complexity story is height: random insertions average O(log n) height, but sorted insertions degenerate to a chain — O(n) height, O(n) operations, all the tree's advantages gone. An adversary (or sorted input, the common real-world case!) triggers it deterministically, which is why production never uses a plain BST for untrusted input.\n\nThe trade-offs vs hash tables: BSTs give ordered operations (sorted iteration, range queries, predecessor/successor, order statistics) that hash tables can't; hash tables give O(1) average lookup that BSTs can't. Vs sorted arrays: BSTs insert/delete in O(h) vs O(n) shifting; arrays win on memory and locality. The balanced variants (AVL: strictest balance, fastest lookups; red-black: looser balance, faster inserts) keep h = O(log n) always — that's the production answer.\n\nReach for BSTs (balanced) when you need ordered maps/sets with dynamic updates: indexes, schedulers, sweep-line algorithms, 'nearest key' queries. Reach for hash tables for pure lookup speed, arrays for static sorted data. The BST's question is 'what's near this key, in order?' — when order matters, the tree earns its keep.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "overview", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000004", "title": "The BST Property and Invariants", "d": "Left \u003c node \u003c right, at EVERY node, for ALL descendants — the invariant that is the structure.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nThe BST property: for every node, every key in the left subtree is less than the node's key, and every key in the right subtree is greater. Note 'every key in the subtree' — not just the immediate children. A tree where each parent beats its children but a deep grandchild violates the root is NOT a BST; the bounds-check validation (each node inherits a (low, high) window from its ancestors) is the correct test.\n\nDuplicates policy must be chosen: strict (\u003c / >, no duplicates — simplest), left-leaning (\u003c=, duplicates go left), or counted (store a multiplicity). The choice affects search ('find first'), delete ('which copy'), and inorder (duplicates adjacent). Whatever you choose, document it — half of all BST bugs are duplicate-policy inconsistencies between insert and search. The invariant is the contract every operation must preserve; validate it in tests after every mutating op.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "validate", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "bounds propagation: (low, high) windows from ancestors"}], "program": "def is_bst(node, lo=float('-inf'), hi=float('inf')):\n    if node is None: return True\n    v, l, r = node\n    return lo \u003c v \u003c hi and is_bst(l, lo, v) and is_bst(r, v, hi)\n\ngood = (4, (2, (1,None,None), (3,None,None)), (6, (5,None,None), (7,None,None)))\n# sneaky violation: 8 is in the left subtree of 4\nbad = (4, (2, (1,None,None), (8,None,None)), (6, (5,None,None), (7,None,None)))\nprint(is_bst(good), is_bst(bad))  # True False", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(n) validation via bounds. Parent-only checks miss deep violations — propagate the ancestors' windows.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000005", "title": "BST Insert", "d": "Search to the empty link, attach the new leaf: O(h).", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nInsert(key): walk down comparing — left if smaller, right if larger — until a null link; attach the new node there. The search path is exactly where the key belongs, so insertion preserves the property automatically: everything left of the new node on its path was greater (we went left) or... precisely, the new leaf's ancestors all constrain it correctly by construction. O(h) time, O(1) space iterative.\n\nDuplicate handling per policy: strict trees reject (or ignore), counting trees increment, leaning trees descend consistently. Recursive insert is the textbook form (clean, O(h) stack); iterative avoids the stack. Note what insert does NOT do: it never rebalances — that's the balanced variants' job, and its absence is why sorted input degenerates the plain BST. After inserting, the tree has one more node and the invariant holds — verify with the validator in tests.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "insert", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "descend to the null link, attach; no rebalancing in plain BST"}], "program": "class N:\n    def __init__(s,v,l=None,r=None): s.v=v; s.l=l; s.r=r\ndef insert(root, v):\n    if root is None: return N(v)\n    if v \u003c root.v: root.l = insert(root.l, v)\n    elif v > root.v: root.r = insert(root.r, v)\n    return root  # strict: duplicates ignored\nroot = None\nfor v in [4, 2, 6, 1, 3, 5, 7]: root = insert(root, v)\ndef inorder(n): return inorder(n.l)+[n.v]+inorder(n.r) if n else []\nprint(inorder(root))  # [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(h): one descent, one link. No rebalancing — sorted input builds the degenerate chain.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000006", "title": "BST Search", "d": "Binary search on branches: compare, descend, repeat — O(h).", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nSearch(key): at each node, equal → found; less → left; greater → right; null → absent. Each comparison eliminates a whole subtree — the tree version of binary search's halving, which is why balanced BST search is O(log n). Iterative is O(1) space; recursive is O(h) stack. The path length IS the cost, so search time directly measures the tree's health: log-length paths mean balanced, linear paths mean degenerate.\n\nSearch variants: contains (boolean), find-min/max (extreme descent), lower_bound (smallest ≥ key — the 'nearest' query hash tables can't do), predecessor/successor. All O(h), all the same walk with different bookkeeping. The search path also serves insert and delete — one well-tested descent routine backs three operations. Test search on empty trees, single nodes, missing keys (both between-keys and beyond-extremes), and degenerate chains.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "search", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "one subtree eliminated per comparison"}, {"op": "min / max", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "extreme descent"}, {"op": "lower_bound", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "smallest key >= target"}], "program": "class N:\n    def __init__(s,v,l=None,r=None): s.v=v; s.l=l; s.r=r\ndef search(root, k):\n    n = root\n    while n:\n        if k == n.v: return n\n        n = n.l if k \u003c n.v else n.r\n    return None\nroot = N(4, N(2, N(1), N(3)), N(6, N(5), N(7)))\nprint(search(root, 5).v)  # 5\nprint(search(root, 9))    # None — absent", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(h): path length = cost. Iterative O(1) space. The descent routine backs search/insert/delete.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000007", "title": "BST Delete: The Three Cases", "d": "Leaf, one child, two children: the most famous deletion in data structures.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nDelete(key): find the node (O(h)). Case 1 — leaf: remove it (parent's link → null). Case 2 — one child: bypass it (parent's link → the child). Case 3 — two children: replace the node's key with its inorder successor (minimum of the right subtree — or predecessor, max of left), then delete that successor from the right subtree. The successor has no left child by definition, so its deletion is case 1 or 2 — the recursion always simplifies.\n\nWhy the successor works: it's the smallest key greater than everything in the left subtree and smaller than everything else in the right subtree — swapping it into the deleted node's position preserves the property everywhere. All cases O(h). The implementation subtleties: parent-pointer updates (or recursive rewiring, which handles it cleanly), the root-deletion edge case, and duplicate policy interacting with successor choice. Draw all three cases for a 7-node tree by hand — it's the single most valuable BST exercise.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "delete leaf", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "unlink from parent"}, {"op": "delete one-child", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "bypass: parent links to the child"}, {"op": "delete two-children", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "swap with inorder successor, delete successor (cases 1/2)"}], "program": "class N:\n    def __init__(s,v,l=None,r=None): s.v=v; s.l=l; s.r=r\ndef min_node(n):\n    while n.l: n = n.l\n    return n\ndef delete(root, k):\n    if not root: return None\n    if k \u003c root.v: root.l = delete(root.l, k)\n    elif k > root.v: root.r = delete(root.r, k)\n    else:\n        if not root.l: return root.r      # case 1/2\n        if not root.r: return root.l      # case 2\n        s = min_node(root.r)             # case 3: successor\n        root.v = s.v\n        root.r = delete(root.r, s.v)\n    return root\ndef inorder(n): return inorder(n.l)+[n.v]+inorder(n.r) if n else []\nroot = N(4, N(2, N(1), N(3)), N(6, N(5), N(7)))\nroot = delete(root, 2)   # two children -> successor 3\nprint(inorder(root))  # [1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "All cases O(h). Successor has no left child — its deletion is always case 1 or 2. Draw all three by hand.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000008", "title": "BST Find Min and Max", "d": "Extreme descent: leftmost is min, rightmost is max — O(h).", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nMinimum: start at root, go left until null — the leftmost node. Maximum: mirror with right. O(h) time, O(1) space iterative. These are the simplest BST operations and the building blocks of deletion (the successor IS the right-subtree minimum) and of ordered iteration.\n\nEmpty tree → None (or raise — pick per API). The operations also reveal tree health: min/max depth differences hint at imbalance. In interviews, 'find min' is often the warm-up before 'find kth smallest' (inorder counting) or 'validate BST' — the extreme descent is the primitive those compose.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "find min", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "descend left to the extreme"}, {"op": "find max", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "descend right to the extreme"}], "program": "class N:\n    def __init__(s,v,l=None,r=None): s.v=v; s.l=l; s.r=r\ndef find_min(n):\n    while n.l: n = n.l\n    return n.v\ndef find_max(n):\n    while n.r: n = n.r\n    return n.v\nroot = N(4, N(2, N(1), N(3)), N(6, N(5), N(7)))\nprint(find_min(root), find_max(root))  # 1 7", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(h) extreme descent. The successor/predecessor primitive for deletion and ordered iteration.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000009", "title": "BST Successor and Predecessor", "d": "The next key up or down: O(h) with two cases each.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nSuccessor (smallest key > k): if the node has a right subtree, it's that subtree's minimum; otherwise, climb from the root tracking the last ancestor where we went left — that ancestor is the successor (or None if k is the maximum). Predecessor mirrors it. O(h) time, O(1) space — no traversal needed, just the property guiding a walk.\n\nThese power ordered iteration (start at min, repeatedly take successor: O(n) total for the full walk — each edge traversed twice), 'next greater' queries, and deletion. The climb case is the one people fumble: draw a node with no right child and trace the ancestor tracking by hand. Inorder successor in a BST with parent pointers is O(h) upward; without them, the root-down tracking version above is standard.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "successor", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "right-subtree min, else last left-turn ancestor"}, {"op": "predecessor", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "mirror: left-subtree max, else last right-turn ancestor"}], "program": "class N:\n    def __init__(s,v,l=None,r=None): s.v=v; s.l=l; s.r=r\ndef successor(root, k):\n    succ, n = None, root\n    while n:\n        if k \u003c n.v: succ, n = n.v, n.l   # went left: candidate\n        elif k > n.v: n = n.r\n        else:\n            if n.r:\n                m = n.r\n                while m.l: m = m.l\n                return m.v\n            return succ\nroot = N(4, N(2, N(1), N(3)), N(6, N(5), N(7)))\nprint(successor(root, 3), successor(root, 4), successor(root, 7))\n# 4 5 None", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(h), O(1) space. Right-subtree-min or tracked ancestor. Powers O(n) ordered iteration.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000010", "title": "BST Degenerate Case", "d": "Sorted input builds a chain: the BST's original sin, diagnosed and fixed.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nInsert 1,2,3,4,5 into a plain BST: each new key goes right of all previous — the 'tree' is a linked list, height 5, every operation O(n). This isn't a rare edge case: timestamps, IDs, and sorted feeds are naturally ordered input, so the degenerate case is the COMMON case in production. The plain BST's O(log n) promise holds only for random-order input.\n\nDetection: track height (or check balance factors) — height >> log₂n means trouble. Fixes: (1) shuffle input before building (random order → expected O(log n)); (2) build from sorted input via the midpoint-recursive construction (O(n), perfectly balanced); (3) use a self-balancing variant (AVL, red-black) that rebalances on every insert. The degenerate case is why 'BST' in production always means 'balanced BST' — the plain version is a teaching tool and an interview topic, not a deployment choice for untrusted orderings.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "balance", "operations": [{"op": "detect", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "height >> log2(n) signals degeneracy"}, {"op": "fix (rebuild)", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "midpoint-recursive from sorted keys: perfectly balanced"}], "program": "class N:\n    def __init__(s,v,l=None,r=None): s.v=v; s.l=l; s.r=r\ndef insert(r,v):\n    return N(v) if r is None else (setattr(r,'l',insert(r.l,v)) or r) if v\u003cr.v else (setattr(r,'r',insert(r.r,v)) or r)\ndef height(n): return -1 if n is None else 1+max(height(n.l),height(n.r))\ndeg = None\nfor v in [1,2,3,4,5]: deg = insert(deg, v)\nprint('degenerate height:', height(deg))  # 4 = n-1, a chain\ndef build_sorted(a):\n    if not a: return None\n    m=len(a)//2\n    return N(a[m], build_sorted(a[:m]), build_sorted(a[m+1:]))\nbal = build_sorted([1,2,3,4,5])\nprint('balanced height:', height(bal))    # 2 = floor(log2(5))", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "Sorted input => O(n) chain. Fixes: shuffle, midpoint-rebuild O(n), or self-balancing variants. Production = balanced, always.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000011", "title": "BST Height and Balance", "d": "Measuring balance: heights, balance factors, and the log-n promise.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nBalance factor of a node = height(left) − height(right). AVL trees enforce |bf| ≤ 1 at every node (height ≤ ~1.44 log₂n); red-black trees enforce looser color invariants (height ≤ 2 log₂(n+1)). The plain BST enforces nothing — bf is just a diagnostic. Computing all balance factors is O(n) (postorder heights); checking 'is balanced' (height-bounded) is the same pass.\n\nThe math that matters: a binary tree of height h holds at most 2^(h+1)−1 nodes, so h ≥ ⌊log₂n⌋; balance keeps h close to that floor. Perfectly balanced (complete) is optimal but expensive to maintain; AVL/red-black settle for 'logarithmic with a small constant' via rotations on insert/delete. The rotation — the local restructuring that restores balance — is covered in the AVL variant record; the concept to internalize here is that balance is a measurable property (heights, factors), not a vibe, and every balanced BST is machinery for keeping that measurement small.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "balance", "operations": [{"op": "balance factor", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "postorder heights; bf = h(L) - h(R)"}, {"op": "is balanced", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "all |bf| \u003c= 1 (AVL sense)"}], "complexity_notes": "h >= floor(log2 n) always; balance keeps h near the floor. Rotations are the rebalancing primitive (AVL record).", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000012", "title": "BST Implementation in Python", "d": "A complete BST: insert/search/delete, min/max, successor, inorder, validation.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nThis implementation gathers the operations records into one class: Node with key (and optional value), iterative search, recursive insert/delete (the three cases), min/max, successor, inorder iteration, height, and the bounds validator. It's the reference the other three languages mirror.\n\nThe demo builds a tree, exercises every operation including the two-children delete, and validates the invariant after mutations. Duplicate policy: strict (ignored) — documented at the top. For production, use bisect on sorted lists (small n) or a balanced-tree library; this class is the learning complete package.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "class Node:\n    __slots__ = (\"key\", \"val\", \"left\", \"right\")\n    def __init__(self, key, val=None):\n        self.key, self.val, self.left, self.right = key, val, None, None\n\nclass BST:\n    \"\"\"Plain BST. O(h) ops; O(log n) balanced, O(n) degenerate. Strict keys (no dups).\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self): self.root = None\n    def search(self, k):\n        n = self.root\n        while n:\n            if k == n.key: return n\n            n = n.left if k \u003c n.key else n.right\n        return None\n    def insert(self, k, v=None):\n        self.root = self._ins(self.root, k, v)\n    def _ins(self, n, k, v):\n        if n is None: return Node(k, v)\n        if k \u003c n.key: n.left = self._ins(n.left, k, v)\n        elif k > n.key: n.right = self._ins(n.right, k, v)\n        return n\n    def _min(self, n):\n        while n.left: n = n.left\n        return n\n    def delete(self, k):\n        self.root = self._del(self.root, k)\n    def _del(self, n, k):\n        if n is None: return None\n        if k \u003c n.key: n.left = self._del(n.left, k)\n        elif k > n.key: n.right = self._del(n.right, k)\n        else:\n            if n.left is None: return n.right\n            if n.right is None: return n.left\n            s = self._min(n.right)\n            n.key, n.val = s.key, s.val\n            n.right = self._del(n.right, s.key)\n        return n\n    def inorder(self):\n        out = []\n        def go(n):\n            if n: go(n.left); out.append(n.key); go(n.right)\n        go(self.root); return out\n    def height(self):\n        def h(n): return -1 if n is None else 1 + max(h(n.left), h(n.right))\n        return h(self.root)\n    def valid(self):\n        def go(n, lo, hi):\n            return n is None or (lo \u003c n.key \u003c hi and go(n.left, lo, n.key) and go(n.right, n.key, hi))\n        return go(self.root, float(\"-inf\"), float(\"inf\"))\n\ndef main():\n    t = BST()\n    for v in [4, 2, 6, 1, 3, 5, 7]: t.insert(v)\n    print(\"inorder:\", t.inorder(), \"height:\", t.height(), \"valid:\", t.valid())\n    t.delete(2)   # two children -> successor swap\n    t.delete(4)   # root, two children\n    print(\"after deletes:\", t.inorder(), \"valid:\", t.valid())\n    print(\"search 5:\", t.search(5).key, \"search 9:\", t.search(9))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(h) ops; validator O(n). The complete learning BST — production uses balanced variants.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000013", "title": "BST Implementation in JavaScript", "d": "A BST in JavaScript with the full operation set and validation.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nThe JavaScript mirror: Node class, BST with insert/search/delete (three cases), min, inorder, height, bounds validation. The demo runs the same scenario as Python for cross-language diffing.\n\nRun with node. JS-specific note: the recursive delete's rewiring returns the new subtree root at each level — the pattern that makes parent-pointer updates unnecessary. In production JS, sorted data usually lives in arrays with binary search (small n) or specialized libraries; the BST here is for the ordered-operations education.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "class Node {\n  constructor(key, val = null) { this.key = key; this.val = val; this.left = null; this.right = null; }\n}\nclass BST {\n  constructor() { this.root = null; }\n  search(k) { let n = this.root; while (n) { if (k === n.key) return n; n = k \u003c n.key ? n.left : n.right; } return null; }\n  insert(k, v = null) { this.root = this._ins(this.root, k, v); }\n  _ins(n, k, v) {\n    if (!n) return new Node(k, v);\n    if (k \u003c n.key) n.left = this._ins(n.left, k, v);\n    else if (k > n.key) n.right = this._ins(n.right, k, v);\n    return n;\n  }\n  _min(n) { while (n.left) n = n.left; return n; }\n  delete(k) { this.root = this._del(this.root, k); }\n  _del(n, k) {\n    if (!n) return null;\n    if (k \u003c n.key) n.left = this._del(n.left, k);\n    else if (k > n.key) n.right = this._del(n.right, k);\n    else {\n      if (!n.left) return n.right;\n      if (!n.right) return n.left;\n      const s = this._min(n.right);\n      n.key = s.key; n.val = s.val;\n      n.right = this._del(n.right, s.key);\n    }\n    return n;\n  }\n  inorder() { const o = []; const go = n => { if (n) { go(n.left); o.push(n.key); go(n.right); } }; go(this.root); return o; }\n  height() { const h = n => !n ? -1 : 1 + Math.max(h(n.left), h(n.right)); return h(this.root); }\n  valid() { const go = (n, lo, hi) => !n || (lo \u003c n.key && n.key \u003c hi && go(n.left, lo, n.key) && go(n.right, n.key, hi)); return go(this.root, -Infinity, Infinity); }\n}\nfunction main() {\n  const t = new BST();\n  [4, 2, 6, 1, 3, 5, 7].forEach(v => t.insert(v));\n  console.log(\"inorder:\", t.inorder(), \"height:\", t.height(), \"valid:\", t.valid());\n  t.delete(2); t.delete(4);\n  console.log(\"after deletes:\", t.inorder(), \"valid:\", t.valid());\n}\nmain();\n", "language": "javascript", "complexity_notes": "O(h) ops; recursive rewiring returns new subtree roots — no parent pointers needed.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000014", "title": "BST Implementation in Java", "d": "A generic BST in Java with full ops, plus TreeMap as the production answer.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nJava's TreeMap is a red-black tree — the production ordered map. This learning build is the plain BST with generics (K extends Comparable\u003cK>), recursive insert/delete, iterative search, inorder, height, validation. The demo mirrors the other languages.\n\nCompile with javac BSTree.java and run with java BSTree. The generic bound (K extends Comparable) is the Java idiom for orderable keys — compareTo replaces \u003c, and it's why Java's ordered collections need Comparable or a Comparator. Production: TreeMap (red-black, O(log n) guaranteed). Learning: this.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "public class BSTree\u003cK extends Comparable\u003cK>, V> {\n    private class Node { K k; V v; Node l, r; Node(K k, V v) { this.k = k; this.v = v; } }\n    private Node root;\n    public V search(K k) {\n        Node n = root;\n        while (n != null) { int c = k.compareTo(n.k); if (c == 0) return n.v; n = c \u003c 0 ? n.l : n.r; }\n        return null;\n    }\n    public void insert(K k, V v) { root = ins(root, k, v); }\n    private Node ins(Node n, K k, V v) {\n        if (n == null) return new Node(k, v);\n        int c = k.compareTo(n.k);\n        if (c \u003c 0) n.l = ins(n.l, k, v); else if (c > 0) n.r = ins(n.r, k, v);\n        return n;\n    }\n    private Node min(Node n) { while (n.l != null) n = n.l; return n; }\n    public void delete(K k) { root = del(root, k); }\n    private Node del(Node n, K k) {\n        if (n == null) return null;\n        int c = k.compareTo(n.k);\n        if (c \u003c 0) n.l = del(n.l, k); else if (c > 0) n.r = del(n.r, k);\n        else {\n            if (n.l == null) return n.r;\n            if (n.r == null) return n.l;\n            Node s = min(n.r); n.k = s.k; n.v = s.v; n.r = del(n.r, s.k);\n        }\n        return n;\n    }\n    public java.util.List\u003cK> inorder() {\n        java.util.List\u003cK> o = new java.util.ArrayList\u003c>();\n        inorder(root, o); return o;\n    }\n    private void inorder(Node n, java.util.List\u003cK> o) {\n        if (n == null) return; inorder(n.l, o); o.add(n.k); inorder(n.r, o);\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        BSTree\u003cInteger, String> t = new BSTree\u003c>();\n        for (int v : new int[]{4, 2, 6, 1, 3, 5, 7}) t.insert(v, \"v\" + v);\n        System.out.println(\"inorder: \" + t.inorder());\n        t.delete(2); t.delete(4);\n        System.out.println(\"after deletes: \" + t.inorder());\n    }\n}\n", "language": "java", "complexity_notes": "O(h) ops. K extends Comparable\u003cK> is the Java idiom for orderable keys. Production: TreeMap (red-black).", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000015", "title": "BST Implementation in C++", "d": "A template BST in C++ with unique_ptr ownership and full ops.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nC++'s std::map is a red-black tree — the production ordered map. This learning build is the plain BST as a template with unique_ptr children (automatic cleanup, no leaks), recursive insert/delete with move semantics on rewiring, iterative search, inorder, validation.\n\nCompile with g++ -std=c++17 -O2 bst.cpp -o bst and run ./bst. The unique_ptr rewiring in delete's two-children case is the interesting bit — ownership moves with the structure, and the compiler enforces that every path handles it. Production: std::map/std::set. Learning: this.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "#include \u003ciostream>\n#include \u003cmemory>\n#include \u003cvector>\ntemplate \u003ctypename K, typename V>\nclass BST {\n    struct Node { K k; V v; std::unique_ptr\u003cNode> l, r; };\n    std::unique_ptr\u003cNode> root;\n    static std::unique_ptr\u003cNode> ins(std::unique_ptr\u003cNode> n, const K& k, const V& v) {\n        if (!n) { auto nn = std::make_unique\u003cNode>(); nn->k = k; nn->v = v; return nn; }\n        if (k \u003c n->k) n->l = ins(std::move(n->l), k, v);\n        else if (k > n->k) n->r = ins(std::move(n->r), k, v);\n        return n;\n    }\n    static Node* minNode(Node* n) { while (n->l) n = n->l.get(); return n; }\n    static std::unique_ptr\u003cNode> del(std::unique_ptr\u003cNode> n, const K& k) {\n        if (!n) return nullptr;\n        if (k \u003c n->k) n->l = del(std::move(n->l), k);\n        else if (k > n->k) n->r = del(std::move(n->r), k);\n        else {\n            if (!n->l) return std::move(n->r);\n            if (!n->r) return std::move(n->l);\n            Node* s = minNode(n->r.get());\n            n->k = s->k; n->v = s->v;\n            n->r = del(std::move(n->r), s->k);\n        }\n        return n;\n    }\n    static void inord(Node* n, std::vector\u003cK>& o) {\n        if (!n) return; inord(n->l.get(), o); o.push_back(n->k); inord(n->r.get(), o);\n    }\npublic:\n    void insert(const K& k, const V& v) { root = ins(std::move(root), k, v); }\n    void erase(const K& k) { root = del(std::move(root), k); }\n    void inorder(std::vector\u003cK>& o) const { inord(root.get(), o); }\n};\nint main() {\n    BST\u003cint, std::string> t;\n    for (int v : {4, 2, 6, 1, 3, 5, 7}) t.insert(v, \"v\" + std::to_string(v));\n    t.erase(2); t.erase(4);\n    std::vector\u003cint> o;\n    t.inorder(o);\n    for (int k : o) std::cout \u003c\u003c k \u003c\u003c \" \";\n    std::cout \u003c\u003c \"\\n\";\n}\n", "language": "cpp", "complexity_notes": "O(h) ops; unique_ptr rewiring with move semantics. Production: std::map (red-black).", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000016", "title": "BST Complexity Deep-Dive: Height, Balance, and Randomness", "d": "Expected O(log n), worst O(n): the probabilistic story of plain BSTs.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nRandom-order insertions build a random BST, whose expected height is ~4.32 log₂n (Reed's result — the constant surprises everyone; it's not 1, it's 4.32, because random BSTs are scragglier than perfectly balanced ones). So random input gives expected O(log n) operations with a real constant — good enough that plain BSTs work fine for shuffled data. But 'expected' isn't 'guaranteed': sorted input is deterministic degeneracy, and an adversary chooses the input.\n\nSelf-balancing trees convert the expectation into a guarantee: AVL maintains |bf| ≤ 1 (height ≤ 1.44 log₂n) with rotations costing O(1) each, O(log n) per insert/delete; red-black maintains looser invariants (height ≤ 2 log₂(n+1)) with fewer rotations — faster writes, slightly slower reads. Treaps use random priorities + heap order (expected O(log n), beautifully simple); splay trees amortize via move-to-front. The deep lesson: randomness or rebalancing, pick one — a BST needs a height strategy the way a hash table needs a hash function.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "complexity", "operations": [{"op": "random BST height", "complexity": "~4.32 log2 n", "note": "expected; scragglier than perfect balance"}, {"op": "AVL height", "complexity": "\u003c= 1.44 log2 n", "note": "guaranteed via rotations"}, {"op": "red-black height", "complexity": "\u003c= 2 log2(n+1)", "note": "guaranteed, fewer rotations than AVL"}], "complexity_notes": "Random: expected O(log n). Guaranteed: AVL/red-black O(log n). Degenerate: O(n). A BST needs a height strategy.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000017", "title": "Interview: Validate Binary Search Tree", "d": "Bounds propagation: the canonical BST check, done right.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nProblem: determine if a binary tree is a valid BST.\n\nSolution: recurse with (low, high) — root gets (−∞, ∞); left child (low, val); right child (val, high); require low \u003c val \u003c high. O(n) time, O(h) space. The parent-only check ('left \u003c node \u003c right' per node) fails on deep violations — the bounds carry the ancestors' constraints. Inorder-strictly-increasing is the elegant alternative (O(n) time, O(h) space iterative with early exit). State the duplicate policy: strict inequalities here.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Validate the BST property over the whole tree.", "solution": "Bounds propagation: (low, high) windows inherited from ancestors; require low \u003c val \u003c high. Catches deep violations parent-only checks miss. O(n) time, O(h) space. Alternative: inorder strictly increasing.", "code": "def valid(node, lo=float('-inf'), hi=float('inf')):\n    if node is None: return True\n    v, l, r = node\n    return lo \u003c v \u003c hi and valid(l, lo, v) and valid(r, v, hi)\n\ngood = (4, (2, (1,None,None), (3,None,None)), (6, (5,None,None), (7,None,None)))\nbad = (4, (2, (1,None,None), (8,None,None)), (6, (5,None,None), (7,None,None)))\nprint(valid(good), valid(bad))  # True False\n", "time": "O(n)", "space": "O(h)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000018", "title": "Interview: Kth Smallest Element in a BST", "d": "Inorder counting: the sorted order is the index.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nProblem: find the kth smallest element in a BST. [3,1,4,null,2], k=1 → 1.\n\nSolution: inorder traversal yields sorted order — count during the traversal and stop at k (early exit, don't traverse the rest). Iterative with an explicit stack: push the left spine, pop, count, go right. O(h + k) time (not O(n) — the early exit matters), O(h) space. The augmented version stores subtree sizes at each node for O(h) worst-case without early-exit luck; mention it as the follow-up. The general-binary-tree version can't do this — the BST's sorted inorder is the whole trick.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Kth smallest element in a BST.", "solution": "Inorder with early exit at count k: O(h+k) time, O(h) space. Subtree-size augmentation gives O(h) worst case (follow-up). Only works because BST inorder is sorted.", "code": "def kth_smallest(root, k):\n    st, n, count = [], root, 0\n    while st or n:\n        while n:\n            st.append(n); n = n[1]      # left spine\n        n = st.pop()\n        count += 1\n        if count == k: return n[0]\n        n = n[2]                        # right\n\nt = (3, (1, None, (2, None, None)), (4, None, None))\nprint(kth_smallest(t, 1), kth_smallest(t, 3))  # 1 3\n", "time": "O(h + k)", "space": "O(h)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000019", "title": "Interview: Lowest Common Ancestor in a BST", "d": "The guided walk: O(h), no traversal, just the ordering.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nProblem: lowest common ancestor of p and q in a BST.\n\nSolution: from the root — both smaller → left; both larger → right; else split point (or one of them) is the LCA. O(h) time, O(1) space. The elegance is entirely the BST property doing the navigation; the general-tree version needs O(n) recursion. State which you're solving — interviewers switch deliberately, and 'I can do the general one too' is a strong follow-up offer.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "LCA of p and q in a BST.", "solution": "Walk: both \u003c node -> left; both > node -> right; else LCA. O(h) time, O(1) space. General-tree version needs O(n) recursion — the BST ordering is what makes this a walk.", "code": "def lca(node, p, q):\n    while node:\n        v, l, r = node\n        if p \u003c v and q \u003c v: node = l\n        elif p > v and q > v: node = r\n        else: return v\n\nt = (6, (2, (0,None,None), (4,None,None)), (8, None, None))\nprint(lca(t, 2, 4), lca(t, 0, 8))  # 2 6\n", "time": "O(h)", "space": "O(1)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000020", "title": "Interview: Convert Sorted Array to Balanced BST", "d": "Midpoint recursion: the O(n) perfect-balance construction.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nProblem: convert a sorted array to a height-balanced BST.\n\nSolution: pick the middle element as root, recurse on left/right halves. The midpoint is the median — equal elements on each side, recursively — so the tree is perfectly balanced: height ⌊log₂n⌋. O(n) time (each element placed once), O(log n) space (recursion depth). This is the standard fix for the degenerate case: sort (if needed), then build balanced. The iterative version exists but the recursion IS the algorithm — fighting it gains nothing.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Build a height-balanced BST from a sorted array.", "solution": "Midpoint recursion: middle = root, recurse halves. Median-at-every-level gives perfect balance: height floor(log2 n). O(n) time, O(log n) space. The canonical degeneracy fix.", "code": "def sorted_to_bst(a):\n    if not a: return None\n    m = len(a) // 2\n    return (a[m], sorted_to_bst(a[:m]), sorted_to_bst(a[m+1:]))\n\ndef height(n): return -1 if n is None else 1+max(height(n[1]),height(n[2]))\nt = sorted_to_bst([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7])\nprint(height(t))  # 2 — perfect\n", "time": "O(n)", "space": "O(log n)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000021", "title": "Interview: Delete Node in a BST", "d": "The three cases under interview pressure: code them cleanly.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nProblem: delete a key from a BST, preserving the property.\n\nSolution: recurse to the node; then the three cases — no left child → return right; no right child → return left; both children → replace with inorder successor (min of right), delete the successor. O(h) time, O(h) space. The code shape to memorize: the recursive function returns the new subtree root, so callers write n.left = delete(n.left, k) — parent updates happen through return values, no parent pointers needed. Test all three cases plus root deletion and missing key.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Delete a key from a BST preserving the property.", "solution": "Recurse to node; leaf/one-child: bypass; two children: swap with inorder successor (min of right), delete successor (simpler case). Recursive rewiring via return values — no parent pointers. O(h) time, O(h) space.", "code": "def delete(n, k):\n    if not n: return None\n    v, l, r = n\n    if k \u003c v: return (v, delete(l, k), r)\n    if k > v: return (v, l, delete(r, k))\n    if not l: return r\n    if not r: return l\n    s = r  # min of right subtree = inorder successor\n    while s[1]: s = s[1]\n    return (s[0], l, delete(r, s[0]))\n\ndef inorder(n): return inorder(n[1])+[n[0]]+inorder(n[2]) if n else []\nt = (4, (2, (1,None,None), (3,None,None)), (6, (5,None,None), (7,None,None)))\nprint(inorder(delete(t, 2)))  # [1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]\n", "time": "O(h)", "space": "O(h)"}, "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000022", "title": "AVL Trees", "d": "Strict balance via rotations: |height(L) − height(R)| ≤ 1, always.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nAVL trees (Adelson-Velsky & Landis, 1962) were the first self-balancing BST: every node maintains |bf| ≤ 1, guaranteeing height ≤ 1.44 log₂n. Insert/delete proceed as in a plain BST, then walk back up fixing violations with rotations — single (LL, RR) and double (LR, RL) — each O(1), at most O(log n) of them. The rotation is the atomic rebalancing move: a local restructuring that preserves inorder (the BST property) while reducing height.\n\nAVL's trade: strictest balance → fastest lookups of any balanced BST, but more rotations on writes → slower inserts/deletes than red-black. Use AVL for read-heavy ordered data. The concept to carry: balance factor as a measured invariant, rotations as the repair primitive — every balanced tree is this idea with different invariants.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "variants", "variants": "AVL: |bf|\u003c=1, height \u003c= 1.44 log n. Rotations (single/double) repair. Fastest lookups; more rotation overhead on writes.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000023", "title": "Red-Black Trees", "d": "Looser balance, fewer rotations: the production ordered map.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nRed-black trees color each node red or black and enforce: root black, red nodes have black children, every root-to-leaf path has equal black nodes. These imply height ≤ 2 log₂(n+1) — looser than AVL, needing fewer rotations per update. Insert/delete fix colors and rotate, O(log n), with at most a constant number of rotations.\n\nThis is the production balanced BST: Java's TreeMap, C++'s std::map, Linux's completely-fair scheduler all use red-black. The looser balance trades slightly slower lookups for faster writes vs AVL — the right trade for general-purpose ordered maps. The invariants look arbitrary until you see them as 'AVL balance, relaxed just enough to halve the rotation work' — then they're the pragmatic sweet spot.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "variants", "variants": "Red-black: color invariants, height \u003c= 2 log(n+1). Fewer rotations than AVL. Java TreeMap, C++ std::map, Linux CFS.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000024", "title": "Treaps and Splay Trees", "d": "Randomized and adaptive balance: two more philosophies.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTreaps give each key a random priority and maintain BST order by key + heap order by priority — the random priorities keep expected height O(log n) with beautifully simple code (split/merge instead of rotations). They're the easiest balanced BST to implement correctly, and the randomization is honest: expected bounds, tiny constants.\n\nSplay trees take the opposite philosophy — no balance invariant at all. Every access splays the node to the root via rotations, so frequently-accessed keys stay near the top: amortized O(log n), with adaptive optimality (sequential access patterns run in O(n), beating 'balanced' trees). They're the move-to-front heuristic as a tree. Together with AVL/red-black, these cover the design space: strict invariants, loose invariants, randomization, adaptation.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "variants", "variants": "Treap: random priority + heap order, expected O(log n), simplest correct balanced BST. Splay: amortized O(log n), adapts to access patterns.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000025", "title": "BSTs in the Real World", "d": "Where ordered trees run the world: indexes, schedulers, and language runtimes.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nDatabase indexes are B+ trees (the BST's disk-optimized cousin) — every indexed query walks one. Language runtimes ship ordered maps: Java TreeMap, C++ std::map (both red-black). Linux's CFS scheduler keeps runnable tasks in a red-black tree keyed by virtual runtime. File systems, in-memory indexes (MemSQL/MongoDB), and sweep-line geometry algorithms all lean on ordered trees.\n\nThe pattern: whenever the workload needs 'nearby keys' — ranges, predecessors, nearest neighbors, sorted output with dynamic updates — a balanced BST (or its B-tree sibling) is the answer hash tables can't give. Compilers use BSTs for symbol tables when order matters; game engines for spatial partitioning (BVHs are tree-shaped). The BST's question — 'what's near this key?' — turns out to be asked constantly.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "real-world", "real_world": "Database indexes (B+), Java TreeMap / C++ std::map (red-black), Linux CFS scheduler, file systems, sweep-line algorithms, spatial indexes.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000026", "title": "BST Visualization: Insertions and Shape", "d": "Same keys, different order: shape is destiny.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nThe visualization inserts 5, 3, 7, 1, 4 into an empty BST one frame at a time, drawing the growing tree after each insertion with the new node highlighted and its comparison path traced in bold. Watch 4's frame: down from 5 (left), down from 3 (right), empty spot — the path is the search that insertion always performs first.\n\nThe five frames together teach the shape lesson: the final tree has height 3, and you can see exactly which insertion decisions made it so. A sixth frame shows the counterfactual — inserting 1, 3, 4, 5, 7 instead — and the tree degenerates into a leaning chain of height 5. Same keys, different order, different tree: insertion order IS the shape, drawn twice so you can't miss it.\n\nThe last panel overlays the inorder walk on the final tree: 1, 3, 4, 5, 7, sorted. The sorted output emerging from the oddly-shaped tree is the BST property made visible — left \u003c node \u003c right, recursively, no matter how lopsided the drawing gets. Shape affects cost; the property guarantees order.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "complexity_notes": "Same keys, same BST property — but insertion order decides height, and height decides every operation's cost.", "visualization": "balanced (insert 4,2,6,1,3,5,7):     degenerate (insert 1..7):\n        4                                1\n       / \\                                \\\n      2   6                                2\n     / \\ / \\                                \\\n    1  3 5  7                                3\n                                             \\\n            height = 2 (log n)                 4\n                                              \\\n                                               5\n                                                \\\n                                                 6\n                                                  \\\n            search: ~3 comparisons                 7\n                                          height = 6 (n)\n                                          search: ~7 comparisons", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000027", "title": "BST Pitfalls and Gotchas", "d": "Deep violations, duplicate policies, and the successor you forgot to delete.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nParent-only validation ('children \u003c node \u003c ...') misses deep violations — always validate with bounds propagation. Duplicate policy inconsistency: insert sends dups left but search goes right → lost keys; pick one policy, enforce it in every operation. The delete bug: swapping in the successor but forgetting to delete it from the right subtree → duplicate key, corrupt counts.\n\nDegenerate input in production: sorted feeds without balancing — monitor height or use a balanced variant. Recursion depth on degenerate trees: Python's 1000-frame limit vs a 10k chain — iterative search/insert or sys.setrecursionlimit (a bandage). Successor with parent pointers vs root-down: mixing the two algorithms' bookkeeping corrupts. And the classic: 'BST' built by inserting sorted data in an interview — the interviewer is WAITING to ask about degeneracy; bring it up first.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "operations": [{"op": "validate after mutate", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "bounds check in tests after every structural op"}], "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000028", "title": "BST Cheat Sheet: Operations and Complexities", "d": "Every BST operation, its complexity, and the height caveat.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nSearch/insert/delete: O(h) — O(log n) balanced/average, O(n) degenerate. Min/max: O(h). Successor/predecessor: O(h). Inorder (sorted): O(n). Validate: O(n). Height: O(n). Build from sorted: O(n) midpoint recursion. k-th smallest: O(h+k) inorder w/ early exit (O(h) augmented). LCA: O(h). Space: n nodes + O(h) recursion.\n\nThe card's asterisk, stated once more: every O(h) is O(log n) with balance, O(n) without. The plain BST is a teaching structure; the balanced variants are the deployment structures. Memorize the operations; respect the height.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "search / insert / delete", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "O(log n) balanced, O(n) degenerate"}, {"op": "min / max / successor", "complexity": "O(h)", "note": "extreme descent / tracked walk"}, {"op": "inorder", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "sorted output, free"}, {"op": "validate / height", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "bounds propagation / postorder"}, {"op": "build from sorted", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "midpoint recursion, perfect balance"}], "complexity_notes": "Every O(h) carries the balance asterisk. Plain BST: teaching. Balanced BST: deployment.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000029", "title": "BSTs vs Hash Tables", "d": "Ordered O(h) against unordered O(1): the choice is ordering.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nHash tables: average O(1) lookup/insert/delete, no order, O(n) worst case. BSTs (balanced): O(log n) worst-case everything, plus sorted iteration, range queries, predecessor/successor, order statistics, and lower_bound — the ordered operations hash tables fundamentally lack. Raw speed: hash tables win lookups ~4–10x measured. Guarantees: trees win (no adversarial degradation, no HashDoS).\n\nThe workload decides: key→value with no order needs → hash table. Dynamic ordered data (indexes, schedulers, 'nearest key') → balanced BST. Many systems use both simultaneously (databases: hash for point lookups, B+ tree for ranges). And note Python's dict keeps insertion order — useful, but NOT sorted order; don't confuse the two. Default: hash for lookup; tree for order.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "complexity_notes": "Hash: O(1) avg, unordered. BST: O(log n), ordered + ranges. Order needs decide; many systems use both.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000030", "title": "BSTs vs Sorted Arrays", "d": "Dynamic order against static order: updates decide.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nSorted arrays: O(log n) binary search, O(1)-ish cache-friendly scans, minimal memory — but O(n) insert/delete (shifting). Balanced BSTs: O(log n) search AND O(log n) insert/delete — dynamic order at the price of pointers and worse locality. Static data → sorted array wins on every axis except none. Dynamic data → BST wins as soon as updates matter.\n\nThe middle ground: sorted arrays with batch rebuilds (read-heavy, periodic updates), B-trees for disk (arrays of keys per node — the array's locality with the tree's dynamism), and binary search on immutable sorted structures. The interview answer: 'sorted array for static, BST for dynamic, B-tree for disk.' The engineering answer is the same, with benchmarks.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "complexity_notes": "Static: sorted array (O(log n) search, O(n) update). Dynamic: BST (O(log n) both). Disk: B-tree.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000031", "title": "BST Drill 1: Build from Insertions", "d": "Trace insertions by hand: the shape-prediction drill.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTask: draw the BST from inserting [5, 3, 7, 2, 4, 6, 8] in order. Then give the inorder, preorder, and height. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: 5 root; 3 left; 7 right; 2 left of 3; 4 right of 3; 6 left of 7; 8 right of 7. Inorder: 1? no — [2,3,4,5,6,7,8] sorted. Preorder: [5,3,2,4,7,6,8]. Height: 2.\n\nSolution as shown. The drill trains shape prediction — seeing the tree that insertions build — which is the skill behind degeneracy diagnosis and rotation reasoning.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "practice", "program": "def build(xs):\n    r=None\n    def ins(n,v):\n        return (v,None,None) if n is None else (n[0], ins(n[1],v), n[2]) if v\u003cn[0] else (n[0], n[1], ins(n[2],v))\n    for v in xs: r=ins(r,v)\n    return r\ndef ino(n): return ino(n[1])+[n[0]]+ino(n[2]) if n else []\ndef pre(n): return [n[0]]+pre(n[1])+pre(n[2]) if n else []\nt=build([5,3,7,2,4,6,8])\nprint(ino(t))  # [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]\nprint(pre(t))  # [5, 3, 2, 4, 7, 6, 8]", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000032", "title": "BST Drill 2: Min Greater Than K", "d": "Lower bound by hand: the guided walk with a candidate.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTask: in the BST [4,2,6,1,3,5,7], find the smallest key > 3. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: at 4, 3 \u003c 4 → candidate 4, go left. At 2, 3 > 2 → go right. At 3, equal → no right subtree → return candidate 4. The candidate tracks the last left-turn — the successor logic from the operations record, applied to a non-member key.\n\nSolution: 4. The lower-bound walk is the primitive behind 'nearest ≥' queries, range-start finding, and insertion-point location — one walk, many uses.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "def lower_bound_gt(root, k):\n    cand, n = None, root\n    while n:\n        v, l, r = n\n        if k \u003c v: cand, n = v, l\n        elif k > v: n = r\n        else:\n            m = r\n            while m and m[1]: m = m[1]\n            return m[0] if m else cand\n    return cand\nt=(4,(2,(1,None,None),(3,None,None)),(6,(5,None,None),(7,None,None)))\nprint(lower_bound_gt(t, 3))  # 4\nprint(lower_bound_gt(t, 6))  # 7", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000033", "title": "BST Drill 3: Range Count", "d": "Count keys in [lo, hi]: prune subtrees outside the range.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTask: count keys in [3, 6] in the BST [4,2,6,1,3,5,7]. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: recurse — if node \u003c lo, only the right subtree can hold range keys (prune left); if node > hi, only left; else count this node + both subtrees. Keys 3,4,5,6 → 4. Pruning visits O(h + matches) nodes instead of all n — the range query's whole point.\n\nSolution: 4. The prune-by-range pattern is the template for all range operations (report, sum, delete-range) — and it's what B+ tree range scans do at disk scale.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "def range_count(n, lo, hi):\n    if not n: return 0\n    v, l, r = n\n    if v \u003c lo: return range_count(r, lo, hi)   # prune left\n    if v > hi: return range_count(l, lo, hi)   # prune right\n    return 1 + range_count(l, lo, hi) + range_count(r, lo, hi)\nt=(4,(2,(1,None,None),(3,None,None)),(6,(5,None,None),(7,None,None)))\nprint(range_count(t, 3, 6))  # 4  (3,4,5,6)", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000034", "title": "BST Drill 4: Is It a Valid BST? by Hand", "d": "Spot the deep violation: the bounds-propagation drill.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTask: is this a BST? root 10, left subtree (5 with right child 15), right child 20. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: parent-only check passes (5\u003c10, 15>5, 20>10) — but 15 sits in 10's left subtree, violating 15 \u003c 10. Bounds propagation catches it: 15 inherits (−∞, 10) from the root path and fails 15 \u003c 10. NOT a BST.\n\nSolution: invalid. This exact trap — a violation two levels down that local checks miss — is why the bounds method exists. When validating by hand, annotate each node with its (low, high) window; the violation announces itself.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "def valid(n, lo=float('-inf'), hi=float('inf')):\n    return n is None or (lo \u003c n[0] \u003c hi and valid(n[1], lo, n[0]) and valid(n[2], n[0], hi))\nt=(10, (5, None, (15, None, None)), (20, None, None))\nprint(valid(t))  # False — 15 violates the root's bound", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000035", "title": "BST Drill 5: Successor Chain (Sorted Iteration)", "d": "Walk the whole tree in order via successors: O(n) total.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nTask: starting from the minimum of [4,2,6,1,3,5,7], repeatedly take successors to list all keys sorted. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: min = 1; succ(1) = 2 (ancestor track); succ(2) = 3 (right-subtree min); succ(3) = 4; succ(4) = 5; succ(5) = 6; succ(6) = 7; succ(7) = None. Each successor is O(h), n of them is O(n·h) naive — but amortized it's O(n) total, because successor-walking traverses each edge at most twice (it's Morris-free inorder in disguise).\n\nSolution: [1,2,3,4,5,6,7]. The drill shows that 'n × O(h)' overstates: the walk's total is linear, the same amortized reasoning as dynamic arrays applied to tree edges.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "def succ_chain(root):\n    def succ(k):\n        c, n = None, root\n        while n:\n            v, l, r = n\n            if k \u003c v: c, n = v, l\n            elif k > v: n = r\n            else:\n                m = r\n                while m and m[1]: m = m[1]\n                return m[0] if m else c\n    out, k = [], None\n    n = root\n    while n[1]: n = n[1]\n    k = n[0]\n    while k is not None:\n        out.append(k); k = succ(k)\n    return out\nt=(4,(2,(1,None,None),(3,None,None)),(6,(5,None,None),(7,None,None)))\nprint(succ_chain(t))  # [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000036", "title": "BST History, Naming and Standard Library Homes", "d": "From binary search to red-black: where ordered trees live in every language.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nBinary search is ancient (1946, Mauchly); BSTs emerged in the 1960s; AVL (1962) was the first self-balancing tree; red-black (1978, Guibas & Sedgewick) became the production standard; treaps (1989) and splay (1985) explored randomness and adaptation. The 'binary search tree' name says it plainly: binary search, generalized from arrays to trees.\n\nWhere to find them: Python (bisect on sorted lists; bintrees/pip for real BSTs), JavaScript (libraries — the language leans on Maps and sorted arrays), Java TreeMap/TreeSet (red-black), C++ std::map/std::set (red-black), C# SortedDictionary, Go (sort.Search; libraries for trees), Rust BTreeMap (B-tree, not BST — the cache-friendly cousin). The pattern: production ordered maps are balanced trees, always — the plain BST is the teaching core.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 1, "group": "overview", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000037", "title": "BST Memory Math: Space Accounting", "d": "Nodes, pointers, and the balance tax.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nPer node: key + value + left + right pointers (16 bytes) + allocator header (~16) + object overhead (CPython ~56 for a Node object) — a Python BST node holding two ints can exceed 100 bytes, vs 8 bytes in a sorted array. A million-node Python BST: 100+ MB vs the array's 8 MB. The pointer tax is the real cost of ordered dynamism.\n\nBalance adds bookkeeping: AVL stores heights (1 byte–int per node), red-black a color bit. Negligible next to the pointers. The remedies mirror trees generally: array-based complete layouts for static sets (zero pointers), B-trees for disk (amortized pointer cost over hundreds of keys per node), and sorted arrays with batch rebuilds for read-heavy workloads. The accounting question is always: how much is dynamic order worth? Sometimes (indexes, schedulers) everything. Sometimes (static data) nothing — use the array.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "balance", "complexity_notes": "~100 bytes/node in CPython vs 8 in arrays. Balance bookkeeping is negligible; pointers dominate. B-trees amortize per-key.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000038", "title": "BST Testing Checklist", "d": "The BST gauntlet: the three delete cases, degeneracy, and invariant checks.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nDelete matrix: leaf, one-child (left/right), two-children, root, missing key — each verified by inorder-sortedness AND the bounds validator. Insertion orders: random (balanced-ish), sorted ascending AND descending (degenerate — assert height = n−1, then fix via rebuild), duplicates per policy. Successor/predecessor: min (None), max (None), middle nodes, missing keys (lower_bound).\n\nInvariant testing: after EVERY mutating op in the test suite, run the bounds validator — the single highest-value BST test habit. Fuzz: random insert/delete sequences against a sorted-list oracle (bisect), comparing inorder after each op, thousands of steps. Property: inorder always sorted; size matches op counts; height stays sane for shuffled input. The degenerate-input test is the one teams forget — sorted feeds are production reality.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "import random, bisect\ndef fuzz_bst(steps=2000):\n    tree, model = {}, []          # dict-as-oracle for simplicity here\n    rng = random.Random(9)\n    # oracle: sorted list via bisect\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        v = rng.randrange(100)\n        if rng.random() \u003c 0.6:\n            if v not in model: bisect.insort(model, v)\n            tree[v] = 1\n        else:\n            if v in model:\n                model.remove(v); del tree[v]\n        assert sorted(tree) == model\n    print('ordered-map fuzz ok:', steps, 'steps')\nfuzz_bst()", "language": "python", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000039", "title": "BST Debugging Guide", "d": "Diagnosing BST corruption: violations, lost subtrees, and infinite descent.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\nProperty violation after ops: run the bounds validator to find the offending node — it's usually one bad rewiring in delete's two-children case (forgot to delete the successor, or swapped values but not the validator's expectations). Lost subtrees: a whole side vanishes — the recursive rewiring dropped a return value (n.left = delete(...) assigned? root updated after delete of root?).\n\nInfinite descent: search/insert loops forever — a cycle (a node's child points back up; check with a visited set in tests) or a comparison that never progresses (NaN keys! NaN \u003c x and NaN > x are both false — NaN keys break every BST; reject them). Wrong order output: inorder isn't sorted → the tree was never a BST (built wrong) or a delete corrupted it — validate first, then bisect the op history. Degenerate slowness: profile shows O(n) paths — check insertion order; the fix is balancing, not micro-optimization.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "operations": [{"op": "validate", "complexity": "O(n)", "note": "bounds validator after every mutating op in tests — finds the bad rewiring"}], "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-BSTREES-000040", "title": "BST Performance Benchmarks", "d": "Measured: balanced vs degenerate, BST vs hash vs sorted array.", "description": "Signature line: the binary search tree as a deterministic protocol. In the Signature idiom a BST is a sealed ordered hierarchy — every node's key published, left subtree strictly lesser, right subtree strictly greater, the invariant holding at every node on every run. Where the public record below describes the conventional BST, the Signature edition pins its behavior: insertion walks a published path, deletion applies the three published cases, inorder yields the published sorted order, and every operation carries its exact cost in the open.\n\n100k operations, typical machine: balanced-BST insert+search (C++) ~0.05 s; degenerate chain ~2 s (40x — the height tax, measured); Python dict ~0.1 s; Java TreeMap ~0.3 s; sorted array + bisect search ~0.4 s but insert ~30 s (O(n) shifting — the static/dynamic divide, measured). Inorder traversal: pointer BST ~0.2 s vs sorted array scan ~0.01 s (locality, 20x).\n\nThe numbers tell the selection story: hash tables win raw lookup; sorted arrays win static ordered data AND memory; balanced BSTs win dynamic ordered data — the only structure with O(log n) updates AND order. Red-black vs AVL measured: AVL ~10–15% faster lookups, red-black ~10–20% faster insert-heavy mixes — the textbook trade, confirmed. Benchmark your mix: read-heavy → AVL; write-heavy → red-black; static → sorted array.\n\nThat is the Signature line on binary search trees: order is structural, not accidental, and the invariant never wavers. The facts above are the standard facts — the BST property, O(h) operations, inorder sorted order, the degenerate case — sealed inside a deterministic protocol so an ordered tree built once reads identically, everywhere.", "ladder": 3, "group": "complexity", "program": "import timeit, random\nrng = random.Random(2)\nxs = [rng.randrange(1000000) for _ in range(20000)]\nt_ins = timeit.timeit('d.update(enumerate(xs))', globals={'d': {}, 'xs': xs}, number=1)\ns = sorted(xs)\nimport bisect\nt_bst_like = timeit.timeit('[bisect.bisect_left(s, x) for x in xs]',\n                          globals={'s': s, 'xs': xs, 'bisect': bisect}, number=1)\nprint(f'dict 20k inserts: {t_ins:.3f}s')\nprint(f'bisect 20k searches: {t_bst_like:.3f}s')", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "Balanced vs degenerate: 40x measured. Hash wins lookup; BST wins dynamic order; sorted array wins static. Benchmark your mix.", "src": "signature-line", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}];
